WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:06.569
For the last seven years, researchers at the
Prakash lab at Stanford University have sampled

00:00:06.569 --> 00:00:12.879
rocks, algae mats, and ocean reefs in search
of a tiny and mysterious multicellular organism

00:00:12.879 --> 00:00:15.240
called Trichoplax.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:21.080
Trichoplax is a species of Placozoa, the simplest
animal at the base of the tree of life.

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:26.650
They don’t have a nervous system, making
them living fossils from a critical time in

00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:28.330
life’s evolution.

00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:32.119
When the researchers looked at Trichoplax
under a microscope, they were shocked to see

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:36.280
its millions of cells engaged in complex activity.

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:41.870
Trichoplax was able to coordinate its movement,
strategize how to find food, and even reproduce

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:43.809
by ripping itself in two.

00:00:43.809 --> 00:00:50.210
But how can Trichoplax exhibit these complex
behaviors without a brain to call the shots?

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:55.650
The answer could shed light on the origins
of the nervous system.

00:00:55.650 --> 00:01:06.010
Traditionally, when you think about intelligence,
you think of the brain.

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:13.220
Trichoplax stretches that intuition, stretches
your imagination to ask how intelligent can

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:17.360
a collection of cells with no neurons, no
muscles actually be?

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:21.430
How much intelligence can a mechanical collective
embody?

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:25.680
To answer these questions, the researchers
tried to understand the simple mechanics of

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:30.830
the Trichoplax, starting with the tiny waving
filaments called cilia.

00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:35.070
Cilia I've often described as the atom of
motility.

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:41.550
For the last 50 years of the work in ciliary
biology and ciliary biophysics, we have thought

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:44.920
of cilia as an actuator that enables swimming.

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:49.690
Typically in organisms with nerves and muscles,
cilia help move fluids within the body.

00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:54.990
But when the Prakash lab studied the cilia
on the underside of the Trichoplax, they discovered

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:56.870
something unexpected.

00:01:56.870 --> 00:02:02.760
I explicitly remember a moment when we started
imaging the organism and literally watch the

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:03.760
cilia.

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:08.640
You could see that what we observed was a
walking gait and the realization was that

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:10.360
the organism does not swim.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:12.240
It actually walks.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:17.360
The researchers designed a mathematical model
to measure the orientation, height, and beat

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:22.080
frequency of each individual cilium as it
balanced torque from its neighboring cells

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:25.560
with the force of its attachment to the surface
below.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:32.040
The result was a regular pattern of locomotion
that the researchers called “walking.”

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:37.570
As they walked, these cilia cells exhibited
dynamics similar to those seen in individual

00:02:37.570 --> 00:02:38.570
neurons.

00:02:38.570 --> 00:02:44.260
When you stimulate the neuron with a voltage,
it executes a spike and that spike passes

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:46.321
information through the network.

00:02:46.321 --> 00:02:51.000
Similarly, what we find in this mechanical
system of a walking cilia where // just the

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:57.170
right pull in the right direction leads to
a transition of a cilia from a stall state

00:02:57.170 --> 00:03:03.150
to a walking state // And it actually starts
“firing” in the sense that it starts “walking”.

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:09.160
But just how these individual cilia cells
coordinated their movement remained a mystery.

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:13.920
We started characterize our work as asking
the question, "How do you walk with a million

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:14.920
legs?"

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:19.569
"How can many of these cilia work together?

00:03:19.569 --> 00:03:23.930
When the researchers looked at all of the
ciliated cells at once, they saw something

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:29.000
they never thought was possible: the cilia
synchronized their movement like a flock of

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:32.180
birds.

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:39.330
To literally be able to watch this kind of
what we end up describing as a flocking dynamics

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:43.320
was surreal because you could see it.

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:46.840
You know, when I see bird flocks I can imagine,
"Aha, there is a controller.

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:50.130
There is a brain in there that's making these
sets of decisions.”

00:03:50.130 --> 00:03:55.740
Here, we are talking about individual flocks
of these in cilia that are seemingly just

00:03:55.740 --> 00:04:04.200
connected by springs and twirling in remarkably
organic ways.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:08.130
You can think about this coordinated activity
like a group of castor wheels attached by

00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:09.620
springs.

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:13.450
Even if they start off facing in different
directions, a force applied to the system

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:18.910
will eventually reorient each individual wheel
so that they all point in the same direction,

00:04:18.910 --> 00:04:21.250
and the system travels as a whole.

00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:26.970
Similarly, each ciliated cell in the Trichoplax
bounces up against its neighboring cells in

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:31.600
a dance between responsiveness and stability.

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:36.720
Individual cells, the researchers found, could
self-assemble into collective systems capable

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:41.960
of a wide range of behaviors, including some
that allowed them to be responsive to their

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:43.400
environment.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:48.760
In other words, basic mechanics could give
rise to the kind of incredibly complex behavior

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:52.420
we normally expect from brains.

00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:58.180
The observation of Trichoplax ciliary flocking
really brought to the fore for us this idea

00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:01.930
that mechanical systems could somehow be intelligent.

00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:04.320
They could somehow seek out food.

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:07.560
They could somehow come to consensus across
many cells.

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:13.389
And they could somehow find ways to keep an
organism alive as a whole, instead of just

00:05:13.389 --> 00:05:14.880
its individual parts.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:19.310
Trichoplax may have set the stage for the
rise of animals with nervous systems by taking

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:27.220
an important first step: embodying simple
mechanics that work together to produce complexity.

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:31.919
One of the implications of our work // is
that it really has pulled us in new directions

00:05:31.919 --> 00:05:36.169
as how we think about computation and intelligence
in physical matter.

00:05:36.169 --> 00:05:39.389
We want to make soft robotic, self-oscillators.

00:05:39.389 --> 00:05:44.831
We want to make caster robots that come to
consensus and work together to make fungible

00:05:44.831 --> 00:05:45.870
cargo transport.

00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:50.980
And we want to think about how we can use
these internal dynamics to make predictive

00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:52.040
machines.

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:57.800
That is a very exciting notion because we
have built an entire world out of passive

00:05:57.800 --> 00:05:59.360
materials.

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:18.470
What if these materials had just the tiniest
sliver of so-called intelligence?

